@@s001 Dynamic programming: the method
@@s002 ora:cell I compute,dato:values already known,ok:answer
@@s003 Items (weight, value): (1, 1), (2, 6), (5, 18). Budget <code>W = 6</code>. <b>OPT(i, w)</b> = maximum value using the first i items with budget w.
@@s004 Row 0: with no items the value is 0. Base case.
@@s005 Item 1 (weight 1, value 1). For each budget: I take it if it fits, value 1 + OPT(0, w − 1), or I skip it. Row 1: 0 1 1 1 1 1 1.
@@s006 Item 2 (weight 2, value 6), budget 2: I take this item: 6 + OPT(1, 0) = 6, against 1 for skipping it. Budget 3: 6 + OPT(1, 1) = 7.
@@s007 Item 3 (weight 5, value 18). Budget 5: 18 + OPT(2, 0) = 18, beats 7. Budget 6: 18 + OPT(2, 1) = 19. Answer <b>OPT(3, 6) = 19</b>.
@@s008 
@@s009 Dynamic programming: the method
@@s010 Dynamic programming (DP) solves a problem by building the optimum from the optima of smaller subproblems, which repeat. Each subproblem is computed once and saved in a table.
@@s011 When DP is needed
@@s012 The natural greedy has a counterexample. But the optimum is built from optima of smaller subproblems that <b>repeat</b>. Signals:
@@s013 an order in which the subproblems shrink: prefixes of a sequence, a DAG, a tree, a pyramid that is followed in one direction only;
@@s014 a <b>numerical budget</b> that becomes an index of the table (knapsack);
@@s015 the greedy fails, but the choices can be ordered.
@@s016 The recipe in eight steps
@@s017 Look at the optimal solution from the end: what is the last decision? (in or out, which arc, which word).
@@s018 <b>Define</b> the subproblem in words, with the range of the parameters: <code>OPT(i, w)</code> for <code>i ∈ {0…n}</code>, <code>w ∈ {0…W}</code>.
@@s019 Check that it is enough: after the decision, is the rest a subproblem of the same type? If not, add a parameter.
@@s020 <b>Recurrence</b>: one case for each possible decision, then <code>max</code> or <code>min</code>.
@@s021 <b>Base cases</b> and edges. An impossible case is worth <code>−∞</code> (if you maximise), not 0.
@@s022 <b>Order of computation</b>: by increasing index, or by increasing length, or by reverse topological order.
@@s023 <b>Answer</b>: which cell you read (<code>OPT(n, W)</code>, or the maximum of a row).
@@s024 <b>Time and space</b> = number of subproblems × cost of one step. If you need the solution, traceback backwards.
@@s025 Knapsack: the table is filled row by row
@@s026 Knapsack and the pseudo-polynomial
@@s027 Knapsack: <code>OPT(i, w) = max{ OPT(i−1, w), v_i + OPT(i−1, w − w_i) }</code> (the second only if <code>w_i ≤ w</code>). Time and space <code>O(nW)</code>: it is <b>pseudo-polynomial</b>, because <code>W</code> is a number and not its length in bits (<code>log W</code>). That is why knapsack remains NP-complete, despite having a DP.
@@s028 
@@s029 
@@s030 The recurring templates
@@s031 Six templates cover almost all the exams:
@@s032 <b>take or skip</b> along a sequence (non-adjacent coins, weighted interval scheduling);
@@s033 <b>numerical budget</b> (knapsack, subset sum, exact change);
@@s034 <b>paths in a DAG</b> or pyramid (Tower, Grave Robber, Bellman-Ford);
@@s035 <b>longest chain</b> in a partial order (after sorting);
@@s036 <b>strings and prefixes</b> (edit distance, words, palindromes);
@@s037 <b>trees</b> (vertex cover on a tree, spreading of a message).
